},{"aggregation_method":"sequence_identity","group_id":"5660_50","similarity_cutoff":50.0,"aligned_regions":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":60,"ref_beg_seq_id":36},{"entity_beg_seq_id":61,"length":66,"ref_beg_seq_id":97}]},{"aggregation_method":"sequence_identity","group_id":"4658_70","similarity_cutoff":70.0,"aligned_regions":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":126,"ref_beg_seq_id":36}]},{"aggregation_method":"sequence_identity","group_id":"5371_90","similarity_cutoff":90.0,"aligned_regions":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":126,"ref_beg_seq_id":36}]},{"aggregation_method":"matching_uniprot_accession","group_id":"P16410","aligned_regions":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":126,"ref_beg_seq_id":36}]}],"rcsb_polymer_entity_name_com":[{"name":"CTLA-4, CYTOTOXIC T-LYMPHOCYTE-ASSOCIATED ANTIGEN 4"}],"rcsb_related_target_references":[{"related_resource_name":"ChEMBL","related_resource_version":"37","related_target_id":"CHEMBL2364164","target_taxonomy_id":9606,"aligned_target":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":125,"target_beg_seq_id":36}]},{"related_resource_name":"DrugBank","related_resource_version":"5.1","related_target_id":"P16410","target_taxonomy_id":9606,"aligned_target":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":125,"target_beg_seq_id":36}]},{"related_resource_name":"Pharos","related_resource_version":"6.13.4","related_target_id":"2054","target_taxonomy_id":9606,"aligned_target":[{"entity_beg_seq_id":1,"length":125,"target_beg_seq_id":36}]}],"rcsb_target_cofactors":[{"cofactor_name":"Ipilimumab","cofactor_resource_id":"DB06186","mechanism_of_action":"Cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen-4 (CTLA-4) is an inhibitory molecule that competes with the stimulatory CD28 for binding to B7 on antigen presenting cells.[A35118] CTLA-4 and CD28 are both presented on the surface of T-cells.[A35118] Ipilimumab is a human IgG1 that binds CTLA-4, preventing the inhibition of T-cell mediated immune responses to tumors.[A35118]","neighbor_flag":"N","pubmed_ids":[18049334,33807678],"resource_name":"DrugBank","resource_version":"5.1","target_resource_id":"P16410"},{"cofactor_name":"Tremelimumab","cofactor_resource_id":"DB11771","mechanism_of_action":"T cell activation is influenced by several processes. T cell receptors (TCR), which are expressed on T cells, bind to the cognate antigen processed and presented by major histocompatibility complex (MHC) expressed on antigen-presenting cells (APC).[A253732] This interaction generates a TCR signal to activate T cells. In addition to the TCR signal, optimal T cell activation requires a costimulatory signal, produced when CD80 and CD86, expressed on the surface of APCs, bind to receptors expressed on T cells.[A253712] CD80 and CD86 are also referred to together as B7 molecules.[A253727] In response to these signals, activated T cells can be further differentiated into specific T cell subtypes with specialized functions.[A253732]\r\n\r\nImmune checkpoints are proteins that control the intensity and duration of T cell activation and response. CD28 and CTLA-4 are homologous receptors expressed on CD4<sup>+</sup> and CD8<sup>+</sup> T cell surface.[A253727] These immune checkpoints have opposing regulatory functions on T cell activity: CD28 is a positive regulator of T cell activity, while CTLA-4 is a negative regulator suppressing T cell activation and proliferation, as well as IL-2 gene transcription.[A253712, A218731] B7 molecules act as ligands to both of these receptors,[A253727] and the balance between CD28 and CTLA-4 expression and signalling influence the extent of T cell activation. In cancer immunotherapy, CTLA-4 has been investigated as a therapeutic target as blocking this receptor can enhance the activation of tumour-specific T cells, allowing them to exert cytotoxic effects on tumour cells.[A253712]\r\n\r\nTremelimumab is an antibody directed against CTLA-4. By binding to CTLA-4, tremelimumab blocks the interaction of CTLA-4 with its ligands, CD80 and CD86, limiting its negative regulatory effect on T cell activation. Inhibition of CTLA-4 leads to increased proliferation of T cells in tumours and promotes T cell-mediated cytotoxicity.[L43652]","neighbor_flag":"N","pubmed_ids":[17673618,33807678],"resource_name":"DrugBank","resource_version":"5.1","target_resource_id":"P16410"},{"cofactor_InChIKey":"PNNNRSAQSRJVSB-KCDKBNATSA-N","cofactor_SMILES":"C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C=O","cofactor_name":"Fucose","cofactor_resource_id":"DB15236","neighbor_flag":"N","pubmed_ids":[37713619],"resource_name":"DrugBank","resource_version":"5.1","target_resource_id":"P16410"}],"rcsb_id":"1I8L_2"}